== Production ==
=== Development ===
Following the release of ''[[Superman (2025 film)|Superman]]'', the first film in the [[DC Universe (franchise)|DC Universe]] (DCU), in July 2025, ''[[The Wall Street Journal]]'' reported that [[DC Studios]] co-CEO [[James Gunn]]—who wrote and directed ''Superman''—was considering making spin-off television series based on supporting characters from the film, such as [[Edi Gathegi]]'s [[Michael Holt (DC Universe)|Michael Holt / Mister Terrific]] and [[Skyler Gisondo]]'s [[Jimmy Olsen (DC Universe)|Jimmy Olsen]].<ref name="GunnConsiderTVSpinoffs" /> Gisondo was expected to return for his own series on the streaming service [[HBO Max]], which would include the comic book villain [[Gorilla Grodd (DC Universe)|Gorilla Grodd]];<ref name="GroddOlsenSeriesJul2025" /> Grodd previously made a [[cameo appearance]] in the [[Creature Commandos season 1|first season]] of the DCU animated series ''[[Creature Commandos (TV series)|Creature Commandos]]'' (2024–25).<ref name="GroddOlsenSeriesJul2025" />

In November 2025, [[Dan Perrault]] and Tony Yacenda were revealed as the writers, executive producers, and [[showrunner]]s of the [[mockumentary]] series, presented as a [[true crime]] [[docuseries]] hosted by Olsen. It follows Olsen and other ''[[Daily Planet (DC Universe)|Daily Planet]]'' reporters as they investigate cases involving supervillains, focusing on Grodd in the first season. Gunn and [[Peter Safran]] were attached as executive producers for DC Studios, with [[Warner Bros. Television]] also producing, and DC Studios executive [[Galen Vaisman]] overseeing the production.<ref name="PerraultYacenda" /><ref name="PerraultYacendaVariety" /> The series was reportedly titled ''DC Crime'',<ref name="PerraultYacendaVariety" /><ref name="CrimeNameDeadline" /><ref name="CrimeNameWrap" /> but Gunn debunked that shortly after,<ref name="NotDCCrimeTitle" /> later stating that it had never been referred to under this title.<ref name="GunnSafranUpdatesJun2026" /> Perrault said in March 2026 that they were deliberating various titles;<ref name="PerraultMar2026" /> one of these potential titles was reported to be ''American Villain''.<ref name="SecondPotentialTitle" /> In June, Gunn described the series as focusing on both Olsen and Grodd, which he clarified was its focus since he and Safran first [[Pitch (filmmaking)|pitched]] it, along with fellow DCU series ''[[Lanterns (TV series)|Lanterns]]'' (2026–present), to [[Warner Bros. Discovery]] before they started working at DC Studios.<ref name="GunnSafranUpdatesJun2026" /><ref name="SeriesOrderEW" />

{{External image|float=right|image1=[https://static.cdn.turner.com/inline-images/daily_planet.jpg An in-universe ''Daily Planet'' press release announcing ''The People v. Gorilla Grodd''], released with the official series order<ref name="SeriesOrderEW" /> – via [https://press.wbd.com/us/media-release/hbo-max-orders-new-comedy-series-people-v-gorilla-grodd HBO Max Orders New Comedy Series ''The People v. Gorilla Grodd''] ({{Webarchive |url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260821190934/https://press.wbd.com/us/media-release/hbo-max-orders-new-comedy-series-people-v-gorilla-grodd |date=August 21, 2026}})}}
The series, titled ''The People v. Gorilla Grodd'', received a formal [[greenlight]] and straight-to-series order from HBO Max in late August. It was set to consist of eight half-hour episodes directed by Yacenda and focus on Olsen and his fellow reporters investigating whether Grodd was wrongfully convicted of murdering his father, the king of [[Gorilla City]].<ref name="TitleOrderCastAug2026" /> Gunn and Safran were interested in further exploring the ''Daily Planet'' characters following their work on ''Superman'' and intended for the series to explore the "darker and funnier" parts of the DCU through the [[criminal justice system]] in [[Metropolis (comics)|Metropolis]]. Perrault and Yacenda wanted to make it "less like an entry into the DCU and more like an artifact from it".<ref name="TitleOrderCastAug2026" /> Gunn described it as DC Studios's "first foray into the true crime genre" and shared an in-universe ''Daily Planet'' [[press release]] announcing the documentary series.<ref name="SeriesOrderEW" /> Gisondo believed the series took a different approach to the superhero genre and universe while still having major stakes. He felt Perrault and Yacenda's work on the [[Netflix]] mockumentary series ''[[American Vandal]]'' (2017–18) made them uniquely equipped for ''The People v. Gorilla Grodd'',<ref name="GisondoNov2026" /> while Wesley Stenzel at ''[[Entertainment Weekly]]'' opined that the combination of the true crime and comedy genres made sense and complemented ''American Vandal''.<ref name="SeriesOrderEW" /> ''The People v. Gorilla Grodd'' is primarily set after the events of the DCU film ''[[Man of Tomorrow (film)|Man of Tomorrow]]'' (2027), with various other time periods also depicted.<ref name="GunnTimelineRelease" />

=== Writing ===
In addition to Perrault and Yacenda, the [[writers' room]] includes Nina Luckshmi Mohan, Mark Stasenko, Zeke Nicholson, the duo of [[Kevin McManus (filmmaker)|Kevin]] and [[Matthew McManus]], Jamiesen Borak, Rachel Hein, Taylor Santiago Berger, and Mackenzie Sammeth.<ref name="WGACredits" />

=== Casting ===
With the series announcement, [[Skyler Gisondo]] was confirmed to reprise his role as Jimmy Olsen, with Gorilla Grodd also appearing. Other reporters from the ''Daily Planet''–outside of [[Lois Lane (DC Universe)|Lois Lane]] and [[Clark Kent (DC Universe)|Clark Kent]]–were expected to appear.<ref name="PerraultYacenda" /> [[Jimmy Tatro]] entered negotiations in early July 2026 to portray Grodd,<ref name="Tatro" /> after previously starring in ''American Vandal'', and he was cast a week later.<ref name="TatroConfirmed" /> [[Mary Holland]] was cast as Sandra, the series' female lead, in early August.<ref name="Holland" /> Casting was finalized ahead of the series order later that month, when [[Eduardo Franco (actor)|Eduardo Franco]], [[Arian Moayed]], Perrault, [[Andrew Leeds (actor)|Andrew Leeds]], and [[Tim Baltz]] were announced as cast in undisclosed roles. They were set to join additional supporting cast members from ''Superman'' who were reprising their roles: [[Wendell Pierce]] as the ''Daily Planet'' editor-in-chief [[Perry White (DC Universe)|Perry White]], [[Beck Bennett]] as ''Daily Planet'' reporter [[Steve Lombard (DC Universe)|Steve Lombard]], and [[Mikaela Hoover]] as ''Daily Planet'' columnist [[Cat Grant (DC Universe)|Cat Grant]].<ref name="TitleOrderCastAug2026" /> In September, [[Sara Gilbert]] joined the cast as a journalist.<ref name="Gilbert" />

=== Filming ===
In June 2026, Gunn and Safran stated that filming for the series would start later that year.<ref name="GunnSafranUpdatesJun2026" /> Yacenda is set to direct all eight episodes.<ref name="TitleOrderCastAug2026" /> In early September, filming commenced at the [[Georgia Institute of Technology]] in [[Atlanta]], to stand in for Metropolis University.<ref name="FilmingStart" /> At the end of October, Gisondo said that filming had [[Wrap (filmmaking)|wrapped]] "a couple of weeks ago".<ref name="GisondoFilmingWrap" /><ref name="GisondoNov2026" />

== Release ==
''The People v. Gorilla Grodd'' will be released on the streaming service HBO Max and will consist of eight episodes.<ref name="TitleOrderCastAug2026" /> It is expected to release after the DCU film ''Man of Tomorrow'', scheduled for July 2027, as part of the DCU's [[Chapter One: Gods and Monsters]] slate.<ref name="GunnTimelineRelease" />

